{"name":"Eccentricity","hex":"#968a9f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eccentricity","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quality of being eccentric or odd; any eccentric behaviour.","The ratio, constant for any particular conic section, of the distance of a point from the focus to its distance from the directrix."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eccentricity"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eccentricity (behavior)","blocks":[{"text":"Eccentricity is unusual or odd behavior which deviates from normal behavior. This behavior would typically be perceived as unusual or unnecessary, without being demonstrably maladaptive. People who consistently display benignly eccentric behavior are labeled as \"eccentrics\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Madame_de_Meuron.jpg/330px-Madame_de_Meuron.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eccentricity_(behavior)"},{"title":"Centre (geometry)","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a centre  or center of an object is a point in some sense in the middle of the object. According to the specific definition of centre taken into consideration, an object might have no centre. If geometry is regarded as the study of isometry groups, then a centre is a fixed point of all the isometries that move the object onto itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Circle-withsegments.svg/330px-Circle-withsegments.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_(geometry)"},{"title":"Eccentricity (mathematics)","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, the eccentricity of a conic section is a non-negative real number that uniquely characterizes its shape.\nOne can think of the eccentricity as a measure of how much a conic section deviates from being circular. In particular:"},{"items":["The eccentricity of a circle is 0.","The eccentricity of a non-circular ellipse is between 0 and 1.","The eccentricity of a parabola is 1.","The eccentricity of a hyperbola is greater than 1.","The eccentricity of a pair of lines is"]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Eccentricity.png/330px-Eccentricity.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eccentricity_(mathematics)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Ellipse_and_hyperbola.gif/330px-Ellipse_and_hyperbola.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Ellipse_and_hyperbola.gif/330px-Ellipse_and_hyperbola.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ellipse and hyperbola with constant a and changing eccentricity e.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEllipse_and_hyperbola.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Cubic_surface.gif/330px-Cubic_surface.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Cubic_surface.gif/330px-Cubic_surface.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ellipses, hyperbolas with all possible eccentricities from zero to infinity and a parabola on one cubic surface.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACubic_surface.gif"}],"sounds":{"eccentricity":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/En-us-eccentricity.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/ce/En-us-eccentricity.ogg/En-us-eccentricity.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Habitable Planet Orbit Eccentricities","image":"https://thumb.wi